{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I have so much on my plate at work right now and I feel like I'm going to drown.", "response": "It sounds like you are carrying a lot, and it is completely normal to feel overwhelmed when the workload piles up like that. You don't have to figure out everything at once. Try picking just the two or three most urgent items and give yourself permission to do good — not perfect — work today. And please remember: it is okay to ask your manager or a colleague for help reprioritizing. You are doing your best, and that matters."}
{"prompt": "I keep putting off starting this novel I want to write because I'm terrified it won't be any good.", "response": "Every writer I admire has felt that same fear, often on every single page. The truth is, first drafts are allowed to be messy — their only job is to exist. Try giving yourself permission to write something you know you will revise or even scrap entirely. Start with a scene that genuinely excites you, no matter how small, and let curiosity lead the way. You are braver than your doubt, and this story is worth your attempt."}
{"prompt": "I said something really hurtful to my best friend and now they won't talk to me. I feel awful.", "response": "It takes real courage to recognize when we've caused pain, and the fact that you're feeling remorse tells me you care deeply about this friendship. When you feel ready, reach out with a simple, sincere apology — no excuses, just owning what you said and how it likely landed. Give them the space they need to process, without pressure. Friendships can weather hard moments when both people are willing to show up with honesty. This doesn't define you as a person; it's a moment you can learn from."}
{"prompt": "I moved to a new city for a job and I miss my family and old friends so much it physically hurts.", "response": "What you are feeling is completely valid — relocation is one of life's quiet upheavals, and that ache is a testament to the beautiful bonds you left behind. It makes sense that it hurts. While you adjust, try holding onto small rituals that connect you to home: a weekly video call, a familiar recipe on Sundays, or a playlist that feels like a warm hug. New roots take time, and it is okay to grieve while you grow. You are building a braver version of yourself, even on the days that feel heavy."}
{"prompt": "Everyone in my grad program seems further along than me and I'm starting to wonder if I made a mistake coming here.", "response": "Comparison is a cruel teacher, especially in grad school where everyone's timeline looks different on the surface. The truth is, you only see people's highlight reels — the published papers, the conferences, the smooth progress — not the rejected drafts, the imposter syndrome, or the days they spent stuck. Your path has its own rhythm, and growth is rarely linear. Give yourself grace where you are right now. The fact that you're here, showing up day after day, already puts you ahead of the version of you that hesitated. Keep going; your timing is just yours."}
